    "math_academy_id": "q-54475",
    "knowledge_point_id": "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/14bd6415062b5e08f183e22059cffa2893442c87c391dfdb1c020c942e15af83.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1,$ as shown above.",
    "worked_solution": "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-1,0]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$",
